WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:01.731
Chinese medicine has a
founding text,

00:00:01.731 --> 00:00:03.539
and it is about two
thousand years old.

00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:05.904
But the version the whole
world reads came from one

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:07.527
man, working in the eighth
century,

00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:09.614
eight hundred years after
the material he was

00:00:09.614 --> 00:00:09.985
editing.

00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:11.661
His name was Wang Bing.

00:00:11.661 --> 00:00:13.516
In seven sixty-two he
found the canon in

00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:13.934
disarray,

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:15.974
chapters out of order and
passages repeated,

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:17.875
so he reorganised it, wrote
a commentary,

00:00:17.875 --> 00:00:19.405
and supplied what he
judged lost.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.690
He added things. To the
founding text of medicine.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.197
And everyone simply kept
them.

00:00:24.197 --> 00:00:25.904
If a clerk did that to my
records,

00:00:25.904 --> 00:00:27.812
he would be out of the
palace by noon.

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:29.820
And this man gets his
name on the front.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.597
He was not a forger, and
that distinction matters.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:34.809
He marked his additions in
a different coloured ink,

00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:36.893
so that any careful reader
could tell them apart.

00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:38.679
Did every later copyist
keep that marking?

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:39.530
Not reliably at all.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.240
Nothing mysterious kept it
alive.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.286
The state paid scholars to
fix its wording,

00:00:44.286 --> 00:00:46.332
printing turned one edition
into thousands,

00:00:46.332 --> 00:00:48.521
and every doctor who
wanted to practise had to

00:00:48.521 --> 00:00:49.330
know it by heart.

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:52.687
A claim need not be true to
be preserved.

00:00:52.687 --> 00:00:54.047
It only has to be useful.

00:00:54.047 --> 00:00:55.898
So what actually deserved
to last?

00:00:55.898 --> 00:00:58.075
The Emperor's Inner
Canon. Chapter One —

00:00:58.075 --> 00:00:59.545
The Sentence That
Survived.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:02.903
A dramatized adaptation of
a classical philosophical

00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:04.166
text. Not medical advice.

00:01:04.166 --> 00:01:06.845
We present the Inner Canon
as history and philosophy,

00:01:06.845 --> 00:01:09.169
and we say plainly where
the evidence does not

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:09.725
support it.
